{title: Leaning on the Everlasting Arms}
{lyricist: Elisha A. Hoffman}
{lyricist: Anthony J. Showalter}
{composer: Anthony J. Showalter}
{year: 1887}
{key: G}
{meta: tune SHOWALTER}
{meta: variation The hymn, sung to SHOWALTER}
{meta: license public-domain}
{meta: tags trust, fellowship}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
What a [G]fellowship, what a joy divine,
[G]Leaning on the [D]everlasting [G]arms;
What a [G]blessedness, what a peace is [C]mine,
[G]Leaning on the [D]everlasting [G]arms.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus: Refrain}
[G]Leaning, [C]leaning,
[G]Safe and secure from all a[D]larms;
[G]Leaning, [C]leaning,
[G]Leaning on the [D]everlasting [G]arms.
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
O how [G]sweet to walk in this pilgrim way,
[G]Leaning on the [D]everlasting [G]arms;
O how [G]bright the path grows from day to [C]day,
[G]Leaning on the [D]everlasting [G]arms.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
What have [G]I to dread, what have I to fear,
[G]Leaning on the [D]everlasting [G]arms?
I have [G]blessed peace with my Lord so [C]near,
[G]Leaning on the [D]everlasting [G]arms.
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}
